    [
        ("Subject pronouns","The core forms are eu, tu, ele, ela, nós, vocês, eles and elas. Portuguese verb endings often identify the subject, so the pronoun can be omitted: Sou estudante. Keep the pronoun when contrasting or emphasising: Eu sou de Luanda, mas ela é do Huambo."),
        ("SER in the present","Ser is irregular: eu sou, tu és, ele/ela é, nós somos, vocês são, eles/elas são. Learn the whole pattern as a set. Do not confuse é (is) with e (and); the accent changes the word."),
        ("Uses of SER","Use ser for identity, origin, nationality, profession and defining descriptions: Sou a Marta; Somos de Angola; Ele é professor. For location or a temporary state, estar is generally used instead: Estou em casa; Estou cansado."),
        ("Agreement","Nationality and many profession adjectives agree with the person: angolano/angolana, professor/professora. A mixed or masculine plural group commonly takes the masculine plural form."),
        ("Forms of address","Tu takes és; vocês takes são. Você, where used, takes the third-person form é. Forms of address vary across Angolan contexts, so observe how people address one another and keep verb agreement consistent."),
    ],
